WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.946
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.946 --> 00:00:02.162
Sunday the eighth.

00:00:02.162 --> 00:00:07.228
An easy one, with brunch on the calendar and the rest of the day wide open.

00:00:07.228 --> 00:00:09.727
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:09.727 --> 00:00:14.726
On a camera, does a lower Kelvin number mean warmer light or cooler light?

00:00:14.726 --> 00:00:16.077
Answer's at the end.

00:00:16.077 --> 00:00:17.360
Weather looks easy.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:27.222
I couldn't get a fresh forecast this morning, but the last one had it sunny and dry, right around sixty, with a steady west wind in the mid-teens.

00:00:27.222 --> 00:00:30.667
That's enough to lean on you if you're riding west.

00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:31.613
Okay, the day.

00:00:31.613 --> 00:00:34.315
Calendar has brunch from nine to eleven.

00:00:34.315 --> 00:00:36.747
After that, nothing on there at all.

00:00:36.747 --> 00:00:38.368
Whole afternoon's yours.

00:00:38.368 --> 00:00:39.314
Alright, news.

00:00:39.314 --> 00:00:40.327
Just two today.

00:00:40.327 --> 00:00:41.745
One from your corner.

00:00:41.745 --> 00:00:47.690
Google put out a new version of its video model, called Gemini Omni one point two Flash.

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:57.147
Clips can now run fifteen seconds, up from ten, and you can keep editing the same clip over a few rounds instead of starting over each time.

00:00:57.147 --> 00:01:02.888
It also got about thirty percent cheaper, roughly seven cents a second for basic H.D.

00:01:02.888 --> 00:01:05.793
The longer clips are the part that matters.

00:01:05.793 --> 00:01:10.048
Ten seconds was barely enough for a product turning on a table.

00:01:10.048 --> 00:01:12.953
Fifteen starts to feel like an actual shot.

00:01:12.953 --> 00:01:14.844
And one from the book world.

00:01:14.844 --> 00:01:21.059
France's biggest literary prize, the Goncourt, just went to a writer named Philippe Jaenada.

00:01:21.059 --> 00:01:26.395
Then he told French radio he'd used ChatGPT three times while writing the book.

00:01:26.395 --> 00:01:34.771
That landed hard, because the jury had already pulled a different book from the running over claims it was written with A.I.

00:01:34.771 --> 00:01:38.149
He says he used it as a helper, not a ghostwriter.

00:01:38.149 --> 00:01:40.648
Nobody's sure yet where that line is.

00:01:40.648 --> 00:01:43.485
Okay, here's the main thing for this week.

00:01:43.485 --> 00:01:48.551
There's a new bakery in Williamsburg called Altbau, on North Eighth Street.

00:01:48.551 --> 00:01:56.725
It's from Chad Robertson, one of the people who started Tartine in San Francisco, which is about as famous as bread gets.

00:01:56.725 --> 00:02:02.264
It's a big two-floor space in an old warehouse that used to be a martial arts gym.

00:02:02.264 --> 00:02:09.762
The country loaf is the headliner, twelve bucks, made from four kinds of flour, dark crust, tangy, soft inside.

00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:12.396
The pastry case is the fun part though.

00:02:12.396 --> 00:02:19.624
A ginger pull-apart thing that eats like monkey bread, and an everything croissant piped with cream cheese.

00:02:19.624 --> 00:02:20.232
Two tips.

00:02:20.232 --> 00:02:26.919
There's usually a line, and what's left depends on when you get to the counter, so early is better.

00:02:26.919 --> 00:02:36.106
And they're stretching weekend hours to ten at night, so next Saturday you could skip the morning crowd and swing by after dark instead.

00:02:36.106 --> 00:02:43.604
Upstairs is supposed to turn into a lounge for classes and events down the line, so it's one to keep an eye on.

00:02:43.604 --> 00:02:45.901
Today's challenge is a making one.

00:02:45.901 --> 00:02:54.141
This afternoon, pick one small thing in your place that's been bugging you and fix it with whatever's already in a drawer.

00:02:54.141 --> 00:03:01.842
A wobbly shelf, a cable that keeps falling behind the desk, a bike bolt that rattles, a strap that's coming loose.

00:03:01.842 --> 00:03:03.126
No buying anything.

00:03:03.126 --> 00:03:05.220
Tape, a zip tie, a screwdriver.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:09.137
Same muscle as rigging a light stand that won't sit right.

00:03:09.137 --> 00:03:11.637
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:03:11.637 --> 00:03:15.419
Remember picking this show's voice off that leaderboard?

00:03:15.419 --> 00:03:20.756
You already know how the scores move, and that close scores are basically ties.

00:03:20.756 --> 00:03:22.242
Here's the next layer.

00:03:22.242 --> 00:03:26.025
What a blind vote actually measures, and what it misses.

00:03:26.025 --> 00:03:27.173
Start with order.

00:03:27.173 --> 00:03:35.819
In any listen-and-compare test, people lean a little toward one slot, often whichever clip they heard last, since it's freshest.

00:03:35.819 --> 00:03:39.534
That's why good arenas shuffle which voice plays first.

00:03:39.534 --> 00:03:42.642
Over thousands of votes, the bias cancels out.

00:03:42.642 --> 00:03:46.627
Then loudness, and this ties right into your minus sixteen.

00:03:46.627 --> 00:03:51.153
If one clip is even slightly louder, most people hear it as better.

00:03:51.153 --> 00:03:52.842
Not clearer, just better.

00:03:52.842 --> 00:03:55.949
Audio folks call it the louder-is-better trap.

00:03:55.949 --> 00:04:02.704
So a fair comparison matches the loudness first, the same way your pipeline evens out every episode.

00:04:02.704 --> 00:04:04.528
The bigger catch is length.

00:04:04.528 --> 00:04:08.108
Those votes happen on short clips, a sentence or two.

00:04:08.108 --> 00:04:11.823
So the leaderboard is really ranking first impressions.

00:04:11.823 --> 00:04:16.349
A voice can be dazzling for ten seconds and wearing by minute four.

00:04:16.349 --> 00:04:20.199
A little too bright, a pet rhythm that keeps coming back.

00:04:20.199 --> 00:04:23.576
Five minutes on a run is a totally different test.

00:04:23.576 --> 00:04:26.549
And last, it's the crowd's taste, not yours.

00:04:26.549 --> 00:04:31.547
The top voice is the one most voters liked, on lines somebody else picked.

00:04:31.547 --> 00:04:34.249
So the leaderboard is a great shortlist.

00:04:34.249 --> 00:04:40.666
The real test is the one you're running right now, listening to a whole episode while you move.

00:04:40.666 --> 00:04:46.138
If anything about this voice has started to bug you, that's the data that counts.

00:04:46.138 --> 00:04:49.313
So: calendar says brunch at nine, then nothing.

00:04:49.313 --> 00:04:51.069
Fix one small thing today.

00:04:51.069 --> 00:04:52.352
The answer: warmer.

00:04:52.352 --> 00:04:54.311
Low Kelvin is the orange end.

00:04:54.311 --> 00:04:56.000
Have a good Sunday, Ziad.
